/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  equil02_methanol_declared_pathways  --  ONE feed, three equilibrium answers:
                                          what a DECLARED reaction list is.

  The same synthesis gas (70 H2 : 25 CO : 5 CO2, 525 K, 50 bar, ideal gas)
  goes to three equilibrium reactors side by side.  A splitter divides it;
  every answer below is read as MOLE FRACTIONS, which do not depend on how
  much of the feed each reactor received, so the split is bookkeeping.

    gibbs     gibbsReactor over EVERY species (CO H2 methanol water CO2
              CH4): no reaction is declared, the mixture finds the minimum of
              G subject to the element balances.  Every pathway the species
              list permits is open.
    declared  equilibriumReactor over a DECLARED subset:
                  methanolSynthesis   CO + 2 H2  <->  CH3OH
                  waterGasShift       CO + H2O   <->  CO2 + H2
              and NOTHING else.  Methanation (CO + 3 H2 <-> CH4 + H2O) is
              left OUT of the list.
    complete  equilibriumReactor over all three reactions, methanation
              included -- a COMPLETE set of independent reactions for these
              six species over three elements (6 - 3 = 3).

  THE PREMISE IS YOURS.  Leaving methanation out states that, over the
  catalyst this reactor carries and at these conditions, methane formation
  is too slow to matter.  That is kinetic knowledge; the engine cannot check
  it and does not try.  It computes what FOLLOWS from it: equilibrium, but
  only along the pathways you declared possible.

  WHAT THE THREE ANSWERS SHOW (measured with this case, 2026-09-27):
    gibbs      y_CH4 = 0.4513, y_methanol = 1.76e-10: with every pathway
               open, equilibrium at 525 K and 50 bar is methane and water.
    complete   the SAME outlet as gibbs (y_methanol 1.76e-10 again, the
               flow 13.14 kmol/h per 25 in, as gibbs has 26.28 per 50): a
               complete set of independent reactions IS the Gibbs reactor.
               So the difference below is the exclusion, not the solver.
    declared   methanol conversion 0.610 of the CO: remove ONE reaction and
               the answer is a different product.
  And the water-gas shift runs BACKWARD in `declared` (the feed carries CO2
  and no water), which the approach knob below reads.

  THE SECOND KNOB.  `declared` carries `temperatureApproach 0;` -- a
  MAGNITUDE the engine gives a sign PER REACTION from that reaction's own
  thermicity as it runs (src/unitOperations/reactor/TemperatureApproach.H):
  methanol synthesis is exothermic and runs forward, so its Kp is taken at
  (T + dT); the shift runs backward, so as it runs it is endothermic and
  its Kp is taken at (T - dT).  Both push against the direction the
  reaction runs.  With 20 K: methanol conversion 0.610 -> 0.442.  The key is declared
  at 0 so the EduTool `declared-pathways` can move it, and every key a knob
  moves sits on its own line (the EduTool's override channel matches a key
  at the start of a line).

  NOT here, and said: carbon deposition (the equilibriumReactor has no
  pure-solid phase yet; the gibbsReactor has one since 2026-09-27 and
  forms graphite in gibbs11_carbon_deposition), non-ideal
  fugacities at 50 bar (every Kp is on the ideal-gas rung; the case declares
  `fugacityModel idealGas`), and any reactor that is not isothermal.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        split;
        type        splitter;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( feedGibbs feedDeclared feedComplete );
        operation   { fractions ( 0.5 0.25 0.25 ); }
    }
    {
        name        gibbs;
        type        gibbsReactor;
        in          feedGibbs;
        outputs     ( outGibbs );
        operation
        {
            T          525 K;
            P          50 bar;
            elements   ( C  H  O );
            species
            (
                { name  CO;        atoms  ( 1  0  1 ); }
                { name  H2;        atoms  ( 0  2  0 ); }
                { name  methanol;  atoms  ( 1  4  1 ); }
                { name  water;     atoms  ( 0  2  1 ); }
                { name  CO2;       atoms  ( 1  0  2 ); }
                { name  CH4;       atoms  ( 1  4  0 ); }
            );
        }
    }
    {
        name        declared;
        type        equilibriumReactor;
        in          feedDeclared;
        outputs     ( outDeclared );
        operation
        {
            T                    525 K;
            temperatureApproach  0;       // K, a MAGNITUDE; 0 = true equilibrium
        }
        reactions   ( methanolSynthesis waterGasShift );   // methanation EXCLUDED
    }
    {
        name        complete;
        type        equilibriumReactor;
        in          feedComplete;
        outputs     ( outComplete );
        operation
        {
            T                    525 K;
        }
        reactions   ( methanolSynthesis waterGasShift methanation );
    }
);
